<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Two Mismatches</div>
<h2>Why Converting AR → Diffusion is So Expensive</h2>
<p>
  Autoregressive models (ARLMs) like Qwen3 predict one token at a time, left-to-right.
  Diffusion Language Models (DLMs) instead start with a fully masked sequence and
  <em>unmask</em> tokens over multiple steps — potentially generating a whole block
  of 4, 8, or 16 tokens per forward pass. That multi-token parallelism is the prize.
</p>
<p>
  Getting there from a pretrained AR model normally requires continuing training on a
  DLM objective, and that process runs into two independent problems that each demand
  a huge number of tokens to overcome.
</p>

<h3>Mismatch 1: Knowledge Retention</h3>
<p>
  An ARLM learns a coherent world model through next-token prediction. Swapping its
  causal attention for bidirectional attention and switching to a masking objective
  can discard that knowledge. The model must effectively "re-learn" what it already
  knew — wasting compute replicating knowledge the ARLM already has.
</p>

<h3>Mismatch 2: Train vs. Inference States</h3>
<p>
  This is the subtler problem. During <strong>training</strong>, a standard DLM picks
  random times t ∈ [0,1] and randomly masks tokens accordingly. But during
  <strong>inference</strong>, the model runs confidence-guided decoding: it unmasks
  the tokens it is most confident about first, then the less confident ones.
  The training distribution and inference distribution are fundamentally different.
</p>

<div class="vp" id="vp-mismatch">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">train-vs-inference mismatch — "The solar system has eight planets"</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">hover tokens to see confidence</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="mismatch-wrap">
      <!-- Training: random masking -->
      <div class="mismatch-panel">
        <div class="mismatch-panel-head red">Standard Training — random masking (t ≈ 0.5)</div>
        <div class="token-seq" id="train-seq"></div>
        <div class="mask-legend">
          <span class="l-mask">masked</span>
          <span class="l-hi">unmasked</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <!-- Inference: confidence-guided -->
      <div class="mismatch-panel">
        <div class="mismatch-panel-head green">Inference — confidence-guided unmasking</div>
        <div class="token-seq" id="infer-seq"></div>
        <div class="mask-legend">
          <span class="l-hi">high confidence (revealed first)</span>
          <span class="l-lo">low confidence (revealed later)</span>
          <span class="l-mask">still masked</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div style="margin-top:.75rem;display:flex;gap:.5rem;align-items:center">
      <span style="font-size:.7rem;color:var(--muted);font-family:var(--ff-mono)">inference step →</span>
      <div style="display:flex;gap:.4rem">
        <button id="infer-btn-1" class="step-nav-btn active-step-btn" onclick="setInferStep(1)">step 1</button>
        <button id="infer-btn-2" class="step-nav-btn" onclick="setInferStep(2)">step 2</button>
        <button id="infer-btn-3" class="step-nav-btn" onclick="setInferStep(3)">step 3</button>
        <button id="infer-btn-4" class="step-nav-btn" onclick="setInferStep(4)">step 4 (done)</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<style is:global>
.step-nav-btn{padding:.25rem .65rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.67rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid var(--border-hi);background:var(--surface);color:var(--muted2);transition:all .15s}
.step-nav-btn:hover{border-color:var(--cyan);color:var(--cyan)}
.active-step-btn{border-color:var(--purple)!important;color:var(--purple-l)!important;background:var(--purple-dim)!important}
</style>

<p>
  Notice the difference: at training step t=0.5, roughly half the tokens are masked
  <em>uniformly at random</em>. But at inference step 1, the model has already
  revealed the high-confidence words ("The", "system", "has", "planets") — and
  what remains masked are the ambiguous or domain-specific ones ("solar", "eight").
  <strong>Those are completely different partial sequences, and the model only ever
  trains on the random ones.</strong>
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Standard MDLM training objective</div>
  <span class="math-var">ℒ</span><sub>MDLM</sub>(θ) = −𝔼<sub><span class="math-var">x</span>₀, t∼U(0,1), <span class="math-var">x</span><sub>t</sub>∼q(<span class="math-var">x</span><sub>t</sub>|<span class="math-var">x</span>₀)</sub>
  [ Σ<sub>i∈masked</sub> (1/t) · log p<sub>θ</sub>(<span class="math-var">x</span>₀ⁱ | <span class="math-var">x</span><sub>t</sub>) ]
  <div class="math-comment">  ↑ trained on forward (random) masked states — never on inference states</div>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s-background" data-code="background">
<div class="section-no">02 — Block Diffusion</div>
<h2>How Diffusion Language Models Decode</h2>
<p>
  Before understanding the fix, it helps to see exactly how a DLM generates text.
  OPDLM builds on <strong>block diffusion</strong> (BD3LMs), which generates text in
  causal blocks. Each block of 4 tokens is denoised independently given the clean
  prefix of all previous blocks. Walk through the 4-step inference process below:
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">block diffusion inference — "The capital of France is Paris"</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">use Prev / Next to step through decoding</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="diff-steps">
      <div class="step-box active" data-step="0">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Initialize — all tokens masked</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Start with a fully masked sequence. The model has no information about the answer yet.
            <br><br>
            <span class="tok masked" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">[MASK]</span>
            <span class="tok masked" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">[MASK]</span>
            <span class="tok masked" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">[MASK]</span>
            <span class="tok masked" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">[MASK]</span>
            <span class="tok masked" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">[MASK]</span>
            <span class="tok masked" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">[MASK]</span>
          </div>
          <div class="step-code">x_T = [MASK, MASK, MASK, MASK, MASK, MASK]</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-step="1">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">First pass — unmask high-confidence tokens</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The model predicts all masked positions simultaneously. Tokens with confidence above a threshold get revealed.
            <br><br>
            <span class="tok revealed" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">The</span>
            <span class="tok masked" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">[MASK]</span>
            <span class="tok masked" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">[MASK]</span>
            <span class="tok revealed" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">France</span>
            <span class="tok revealed" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">is</span>
            <span class="tok masked" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">[MASK]</span>
          </div>
          <div class="step-code">x_t2 = ["The", MASK, MASK, "France", "is", MASK]</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-step="2">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Second pass — unmask remaining ambiguous tokens</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Now conditioned on the already-revealed tokens, the model resolves the remaining positions. Context narrows the choices.
            <br><br>
            <span class="tok revealed" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">The</span>
            <span class="tok lo" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">capital</span>
            <span class="tok lo" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">of</span>
            <span class="tok revealed" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">France</span>
            <span class="tok revealed" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">is</span>
            <span class="tok masked" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">[MASK]</span>
          </div>
          <div class="step-code">x_t1 = ["The", "capital", "of", "France", "is", MASK]</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-step="3">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Final pass — fully decoded sequence</div>
          <div class="step-desc">All positions unmasked. The full sequence is complete. Multiple tokens were generated per forward pass — this is the DLM's parallelism advantage.
            <br><br>
            <span class="tok revealed" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">The</span>
            <span class="tok revealed" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">capital</span>
            <span class="tok revealed" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">of</span>
            <span class="tok revealed" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">France</span>
            <span class="tok revealed" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">is</span>
            <span class="tok hi" style="display:inline-flex;margin:.1rem">Paris</span>
          </div>
          <div class="step-code">x_0 = ["The", "capital", "of", "France", "is", "Paris"] ✓</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="diff-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <button id="diff-next">Next →</button>
      <span class="step-prog" id="diff-prog">Step 1 of 4</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  Key insight: the intermediate states at step 2 and step 3 — e.g. <code>["The", MASK, MASK, "France", "is", MASK]</code> — are the states the model <strong>actually encounters during inference</strong>. Standard training never shows the model these states. That is exactly the mismatch OPDLM is designed to fix.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s-method" data-code="method">
<div class="section-no">03 — OPDLM Framework</div>
<h2>Train on the States You'll Actually See</h2>
<p>
  OPDLM's central idea is elegant: instead of training the DLM student on random
  forward-masked states, <strong>run the student's own inference process and train
  on those states</strong>. This eliminates the train-inference gap by definition.
</p>
<p>
  But there's a catch: to provide supervision on a diffusion state, you normally need
  a <em>diffusion teacher</em> — which is what you're trying to build in the first
  place. OPDLM's key insight: use the original frozen ARLM as the teacher, querying
  it on the <em>terminal (fully decoded) sequence</em> x₀ rather than on the
  partially masked state x<sub>t</sub>. That way, no DLM teacher is needed.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">OPDLM training loop — one step</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">walk through Algorithm 1</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="training-flow">
      <div class="tf-row">
        <span class="tf-label">student</span>
        <span class="tf-content">
          <span class="tf-box student">DLM pθ</span>
          <span class="tf-arrow">→ sample reverse trajectory →</span>
          <span class="tf-box traj">(x_T, x_&#123;T-1&#125;, …, x_0)</span>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div class="tf-row">
        <span class="tf-label">pick state</span>
        <span class="tf-content">
          <span class="tf-arrow">from trajectory, sample one index k →</span>
          <span class="tf-box state">x_t = τ(t_k)</span>
          <span class="tf-arrow">(partially masked)</span>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div class="tf-row">
        <span class="tf-label">teacher</span>
        <span class="tf-content">
          <span class="tf-box teacher">frozen ARLM pφ</span>
          <span class="tf-arrow">reads clean prefix x₀ →</span>
          <span class="tf-box teacher">p_φ(· | x₀&lt;b, x₀^b,&lt;i)</span>
        </span>
      </div>
      <div class="tf-row">
        <span class="tf-label">loss</span>
        <span class="tf-content">
          <span class="tf-arrow">for each masked position i in block b →</span>
          <span class="tf-box loss">KL(teacher ‖ student)</span>
        </span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="math-block" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <div class="math-label">OPDLM objective</div>
      <span class="math-var">ℒ</span><sub>OPDLM</sub>(θ) = 𝔼<sub>τ∼Γ<sub>θ</sub><sup>rev</sup></sub> [ Σ<sub>b,i∈masked</sub> w(t) · KL( p<sub>φ</sub>(·|<span class="math-var">x</span>₀<sup>&lt;b</sup>, <span class="math-var">x</span>₀<sup>b,&lt;i</sup>) ‖ p<sub>θ</sub>(·|<span class="math-var">x</span>₀<sup>&lt;b</sup>, <span class="math-var">x</span><sub>t</sub><sup>b</sup>) ) ]
      <div class="math-comment">  τ ~ reverse trajectory of the student — same process as inference</div>
      <div class="math-comment">  teacher uses clean terminal sequence x₀, not the masked state x_t</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>The Rollout-Length Curriculum</h3>
<p>
  When training starts, the student has ARLM weights but bidirectional attention — a mode
  it has never been run in before. Early rollouts produce incoherent sequences, which
  would give the teacher garbage to supervise on. OPDLM adds a <strong>curriculum</strong>:
  start with short sequences (so the teacher context is mostly the clean prompt, which it
  knows well), then gradually increase rollout length as the student improves.
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Rollout-length curriculum</div>
  <span class="math-var">L</span><sub>s</sub> = min( L<sub>max</sub>,  L<sub>min</sub> + ⌊ s / S<sub>warm</sub> · (L<sub>max</sub> − L<sub>min</sub>) ⌋ )
  <div class="math-comment">  at step s: linearly ramp from L_min to L_max over S_warm warmup steps</div>
</div>

<p>
  Training runs on only ~60K prompt samples across math, code, science, and chat.
  Because OPDLM is <em>on-policy</em>, only the prompts are needed — the student
  generates its own responses. Total training: 0.066B–0.076B tokens.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s-ablation" data-code="ablation">
<div class="section-no">04 — Why On-Policy Wins</div>
<h2>On-Policy vs Off-Policy: A Controlled Comparison</h2>
<p>
  The paper carefully decomposes the benefit of OPDLM into two components with a
  controlled ablation at matched data and compute. Three variants are compared, all
  trained on the same prompt corpus for one epoch:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Off-Policy</strong> — standard KD: teacher generates responses offline, student trains on those with random masking</li>
  <li><strong>OPDLM<sub>off</sub></strong> — hybrid: student generates its own responses (on-policy response), but applies random masking (off-policy masking)</li>
  <li><strong>OPDLM</strong> — fully on-policy: both the response and the masking trajectory come from the student's inference</li>
</ul>

